      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i'm looking at the resource limits now and they seem fine, not hitting OOMKilled. i also checked the probes and they look okay on paper. i'm gonna try running `kubectl logs --previous` to see if the app is throwing an error before it dies. \n\none more thing—is there any way to tell if the issue is happening at the container entrypoint level vs something happening internally in the app logic after it starts up? like if the container starts but then immediately crashes because of a bad env var or something?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To determine if the issue is happening at the container entrypoint level or within the app logic:\n\n- Check your Entrypoints and Execs in your Dockerfile. Modify your startup command to print \"hello world\" by replacing the current command with `echo \"hello world\"`. If this prints successfully before any crash, then your entrypoint is functioning correctly, indicating the problem lies after startup.\n- Review your environment variables; it's possible there may be an invalid setting. Cross-reference with your manifest to ensure all necessary variables are properly passed down.\n- Examine the init container, if any, as it might already be contributing to the crash. Pay attention to any startup issues or logs that can offer clues.\n- Look into the Kubernetes' Init Containers to confirm they are completing their tasks before the main container starts. Sometimes delay in an init container can cause resource contention or deadlocks.\n- Perform a rolling restart of the auth-service pods using `kubectl rollout restart deployment <deployment-name>` to isolate if the issue is persistent across all pods or isolated to specific instances.\n- Examine the Docker manifest directly `docker manifest <manifest-name>` to validate that it was built and pushed correctly, and changes are propagated.\n- Lastly, trace the Signal Number (`--severity",
      "source": "target",
